WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:06.910
Speaker 1: Let's start today with some meaty
policy analysis when it comes to taxing the

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:07.910
rich.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:08.910
Please don't tune out.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:09.910
No, no, no, don't click away.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:10.910
No.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.580
In all seriousness, this morning, I watched
a little bit of our friend Sam Sanders appearance

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.920
on the Patrick Bat David Show, a program I
did a month or six weeks ago or so.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.720
And one of the topics that came up with them
was about what should tax rates be and taxing

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:26.720
the rich.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.070
And Sam says yes and Patrick says no.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:35.480
And okay, I don't want in this segment to
delve into the should we or shouldn't we or

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.760
the morals of the ethics.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:44.850
What I simply want to address here is if we
wanted to tax the ultra wealthy more, how

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.380
would we do it effectively?

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:52.280
And what I mean by effectively is it's very
easy to say raise the top income tax rate.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.399
But then you realize, wait a second, a lot
of the ultra wealthy aren't earning income

00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:59.470
in wages subject to that rate.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.220
So that's not really going to help.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:06.110
And in the background of all of this is the
news that Democratic senators, as HuffPost

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:11.960
reports, are urging the Biden administration
to go after what they call ultra wealthy tax

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.220
dodgers.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.610
Most of this is specifically related to using
trusts and other financial vehicles when it

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.180
comes to inheritance, when it comes to inheritance
and what we might call estate tax or some

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.229
like to call it the death tax.

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:29.680
So what I want to do in this segment, just
to provide a little bit of food for thought,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:37.020
is give a bunch of different changes that
could be made to the tax code that would help

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.430
tax the ultra wealthy.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:43.520
Now, I know that many of you are going to
write to me and you're going to say, But,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.610
David, that method wouldn't work because of
this reason, and this other method wouldn't

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.939
work because of that other reason.

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:56.860
This is very much like when we look at an
issue like gun violence, no one policy would

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.619
cover all mass shootings and prevent every
single one.

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:08.420
When we talk about waiting periods, mandatory
liability insurance, limiting certain firearms

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:14.349
only to those over age 25, requiring relicensing
and more extensive training.

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:17.599
We have this list that I've put together of
10 to 12 different items that could be put

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:19.080
in place.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.170
No single one would prevent every mass shooting.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:27.660
And in a country of 330 million people, 340
million people, no matter what you do, there

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.459
might still be some mass shootings.

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:33.450
But the point is, what's the lowest hanging
fruit?

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:40.220
What can we do to, in the case of the tax
element, collect more from the ultra wealthy?

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.690
So here is the list and I'll tell you how
they would work and their limits.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.440
And again, no, there is no silver bullet here.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.440
So.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.610
Okay, should we raise the top marginal income
tax rate?

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.310
Yeah, we probably should.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.080
Saying that at I don't remember where it is
right now.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.360
550,000 or so dollars a year.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.370
You hit the top tax rate of 37%.

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:04.120
And from there up there's no more brackets
and no higher marginal tax rates.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.380
That's probably too low.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:11.170
There should probably be some more brackets
with some higher rates, but that won't get

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:17.319
us all of that ultra rich tax revenue that
we might be seeking because a lot of the ultra

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:20.330
wealthy aren't earning money via wages.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:24.989
They're also able to reduce taxes through
deductions and tax credits, etc..

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:28.120
Okay, So one tool is you raise the top marginal
tax rate.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.739
It's not going to do everything you want it
to do.

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:37.390
Secondly, you can look at imposing a wealth
tax on certain assets.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:42.260
Now that is going to it's quite difficult
to implement.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:46.640
I'm honestly not even completely sure that
it would pass all legal muster, but it's an

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:47.890
idea that's been proposed.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:53.769
You impose a wealth tax to try to get at taxation
of assets rather than just income.

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:55.409
Now, what are the problems?

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:58.629
Well, what do you do about illiquid assets?

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:01.560
Do you tax the value of fine art?

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.800
Who determines what the fine art is worth?

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.060
You probably have to exclude an asset like
fine art.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:13.680
And so what this leads to is that those who
hold valuable assets can either move assets

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.120
to countries with no wealth taxes, invest
in assets that are difficult to value or would

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.690
be exempted like artwork or rare collectibles.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.710
Okay, but it might do something.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:28.050
And to be clear, I'm not necessarily advocating
for every single one of these policies.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.920
I'm telling you what is on the what is in
the toolbox, so to speak.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.840
Number three, there are tax loopholes that
could be closed.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:41.600
One such loophole is the so-called carried
interest loophole, however.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:47.250
There would be some ultra wealthy folks who
will find ways to get around that either moving

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:52.240
the types of assets that would have previously
benefit benefited from the carried interest

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.850
loophole into nontaxable assets like municipal
bonds.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.620
Not totally realistic for the hedge fund managers
that that would be targeted at.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:06.880
Admittedly looking at changing business structure,
moving certain assets offshore, lobbying for

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.460
a new different loophole the next time there's
a friendly administration.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.090
So is it a panacea?

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.090
No.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:14.190
But is it a tool?

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.190
Absolutely.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:21.010
Number four, increasing IRS funding to do
more audits of the ultra wealthy.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:28.479
Now, there are some people in that income
bracket that are doing everything right.

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:33.970
It's not like with all of these folks, if
they were audited, the government would find

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.060
that they have underpaid their taxes.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:42.889
So for those who are legally and correctly
avoiding taxes, auditing them isn't going

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:43.889
to make a difference.

00:05:43.889 --> 00:05:49.050
But we know that there is significant underpayment
in that group and having a more well-funded

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:57.110
IRS not with agents with guns, just audit
agents to do audits would certainly claw back

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:02.410
some of that revenue that should have been
paid a financial transaction tax.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:07.400
If you put in place a financial transaction
tax, the ultra wealthy who might not earn

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:13.880
any income and pay very little as far as income
taxes go but are involved in financial transactions,

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:18.090
they would have to pay a tax on those transactions.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:23.550
And as with anything else, they could look
at stopping the financial transactions in

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.740
order to not generate those taxable events.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:32.099
Moving assets offshore to countries that don't
have such a tax or investing in assets not

00:06:32.099 --> 00:06:34.840
subjected to that tax certainly could do it.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:39.970
But it is another tool raising capital gains.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:46.100
If you are worried about not collecting tax
revenue from the ultra wealthy because they

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:51.210
are earning money that is subject to capital
gains rather than income tax, you could raise

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.400
the tax on capital gains.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:54.700
It's not a silver bullet.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:07:01.289
You could avoid that tax as an ultra rich
person by waiting to take any gains or through

00:07:01.289 --> 00:07:03.360
other mechanisms.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.380
An alternative minimum tax.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:12.030
Alternative minimum tax says, listen, if this
is your income before deductions, no matter

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:18.319
how many deductions you take, there is a minimum
level of tax that you have to pay.

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:23.039
You can't deduct and credit your way all the
way down below some certain amount.

00:07:23.039 --> 00:07:24.400
That's a good idea.

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.920
High earners might still be able to reduce
their taxable income significantly through

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:32.390
the tools that do remain available.

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:33.879
But it's a it's a it's a tool.

00:07:33.879 --> 00:07:35.810
The alternative minimum tax.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:38.440
You could look at increasing corporate taxes.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:39.950
That is a great idea.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:44.729
That being said, you have to remember that
that only applies to certain types of corporations

00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:45.740
as corporations.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:47.930
An LLC is in sole proprietorships.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:52.580
Those are what are called pass through type
entities, and those are governed by the personal

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:56.970
income tax rate, not by the corporate tax
rate, but certainly it would make a difference.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:02.550
So these are all tools that are available,
every single one.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:09.949
Is at risk of being circumvented, at least
partially, depending on what tax methods and

00:08:09.949 --> 00:08:11.430
strategies you use.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:17.300
But taken together, much like doing ten or
12 different things about gun safety, it would

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.100
have a significant impact in its totality.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:25.129
Now, this is a separate issue from, as I discussed
on the Patrick Bat David Show.

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:29.770
I'm not immediately looking to just raise
all sorts of taxes.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:34.899
What I think is the really the lowest hanging
fruit is number one, what are we getting for

00:08:34.899 --> 00:08:36.919
the taxes we already pay?

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:38.839
What are the taxes being used for?

00:08:38.839 --> 00:08:45.910
And secondly, getting actually closer to the
tax rates that are in place simply by focusing

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:47.660
on deductions, credits and loopholes.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.779
But that's a subject subject for a different
day.

00:08:50.779 --> 00:08:52.640
Last item about this.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:55.640
Any time that there's a tax proposal.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:59.600
And you hear, oh, this would raise 10 billion
or whatever the case may be.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.790
It's really important to understand whether
that estimate of this would generate $10 billion

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:05.270
in tax revenue.

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:08.080
Is it based on a dynamic scoring system?

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:13.210
And what I mean by that is it's very easy
to say based on the way people filed their

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:19.019
taxes last year, if we put in place this new
tax, it would generate $10 billion.

00:09:19.019 --> 00:09:25.990
What needs to be taken into account is that
last year, if this tax had been in place,

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:30.950
people might have used a different tax strategy
in response to the fact that that tax was

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:31.950
put in place.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:37.070
So whenever we examine this type of proposal
of any kind when it comes to taxes, you want

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:42.380
to dynamically score what it would do by accounting
for how people's behavior might change.

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:43.880
Send me your thoughts.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:45.860
Is this interesting to people?

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:46.020
Let me know.

